 185:memory.c      **** /*Step 1: Calculate the page number(page_number)
 186:memory.c      **** c
 187:memory.c      **** unsigned long page_number = p->PHY_address >> PAGE_2M_SHIFT;
 188:memory.c      **** The physical address is shifted 21 bits to the right (since 2MB = 2^21 bytes)
 189:memory.c      **** 
 190:memory.c      **** Obtain the index number of the page in the global context
 191:memory.c      **** Step 2: Calculate the bitmap array index (word_index)
 192:memory.c      **** c
 193:memory.c      **** unsigned long word_index = page_number >> 6;  // Equivalent to page_number / 64
 194:memory.c      **** Each unsigned long has 64 bits (for 64-bit systems)
 195:memory.c      **** An unsigned long can manage 64 pages.
 196:memory.c      **** Step 3: Calculate bit offset（bit_offset）
 197:memory.c      **** c
 198:memory.c      **** unsigned long bit_offset = page_number % 64;
 199:memory.c      **** The specific location of the page in the bitmap unsigned long data structure
 200:memory.c      **** example
 201:memory.c      **** 
 202:memory.c      **** text
GAS LISTING memory.s 			page 96


 203:memory.c      **** page_number = 129
 204:memory.c      **** bit_offset = 129 % 64 = 1 (First place, counting from 0)
 205:memory.c      **** 
 206:memory.c      **** Step 4: Construct the bit mask and perform the XOR operation
 207:memory.c      **** c
 208:memory.c      **** 1UL << bit_offset  // Create a mask where only the bit at the position of bit_offset is set to 1.
 209:memory.c      **** ^=                 // Exclusive OR operation, flipping the designated bit position
 210:memory.c      **** Complete calculation process:
 211:memory.c      **** 
 212:memory.c      **** text
 213:memory.c      **** assume：
 214:memory.c      **** bits_map address = 0x12346000
 215:memory.c      **** page_number = 129
 216:memory.c      **** 
 217:memory.c      **** caculate ：
 218:memory.c      **** 1. word_index = 129 >> 6 = 2
 219:memory.c      **** 2. Bitmap position = bits_map + 2 = 0x12346000 + 2*8 = 0x12346010
 220:memory.c      **** 3. bit_offset = 129 % 64 = 1
 221:memory.c      **** 4. mask = 1UL << 1 = 0x2 (二进制 10)
 222:memory.c      **** 5. Exclusive OR operation：bits_map[2] ^= 0x2
 223:memory.c      **** 
 224:memory.c      **** 4. Why use the exclusive OR operation ( ^= )? Background:
 225:memory.c      **** 
 226:memory.c      **** The bitmap is initialized with all values set to 0xFF (full 1s): All pages are marked as "occupied"
 227:memory.c      **** The available pages need to be marked as "idle" (0).
 228:memory.c      **** 		 */		
